 <tr><td>Wholesale baselines</td><td>8 × €7k = 56</td><td>32 × €7k = 224</td></tr>
 <tr><td>Recurring wholesale operations</td><td>4 clients × 6 months × €2.4k = 57.6</td><td>24 clients × €28.8k = 691.2</td></tr>
 <tr><td><strong>Revenue</strong></td><td><strong>113.6</strong></td><td><strong>915.2</strong></td></tr>
 <tr><td>Pass-through labs/hardware</td><td>0</td><td>0</td></tr>
-<tr><td>Delivery labour, specialist review, tools</td><td>28</td><td>400</td></tr>
+<tr><td>Delivery labour</td><td>12</td><td>334: 4 analysts × 62 + 1 senior × 86</td></tr>
+<tr><td>Specialist review</td><td>8</td><td>30</td></tr>
+<tr><td>Delivery tools</td><td>8</td><td>36</td></tr>
 <tr><td><strong>Gross margin</strong></td><td><strong>85.6 (75%)</strong></td><td><strong>515.2 (56%)</strong></td></tr>
 <tr><td>Partner enablement, travel, insurance, legal, admin</td><td>18</td><td>155</td></tr>
 <tr><td>Founder labour at replacement cost</td><td>85</td><td>105</td></tr>
 <tr><td><strong>Operating surplus after founder replacement</strong></td><td><strong>−17.4</strong></td><td><strong>255.2</strong></td></tr>
+<tr><td>Capital expenditure, cash not profit-and-loss</td><td>18</td><td>8 annual refresh</td></tr>
 </tbody></table></div>
 <p>Year one does not fund the owner target. The mature model does, but its apparent scale hides two ratios. Eight producing partners must each create four baseline projects a year. Three quarters of those baselines must convert or coexist as twenty-four recurring end customers. A partner that signs an agreement but produces no paid work is not a channel and does not belong in the denominator.</p>
-<p>The direct-cost line allows four loaded delivery roles plus specialist review and tooling. Founder compensation of €105,000 is priced as replacement labour, not surplus. The €255,200 remainder is pre-tax company surplus for reserves, hiring and investment. Recurring revenue is €691,200, 76% of the total, but concentration matters more: if two partners source half the clients, they can renegotiate the wholesale rate or threaten to move the book.</p>
-<p>Partner payment terms can create a cash gap even when the end customer prepays. If the partner pays at 45 or 60 days while staff are monthly, the mature operation can need more than €100,000 of payroll and tax liquidity. The launch terms must therefore prohibit “pay when paid,” require the booking deposit, stop work on overdue invoices and keep laboratory spend outside the company. Do not use the reader's capital to finance a better-funded consultancy's receivables.</p>
+<div class="ledger-wrap"><table class="ledger">
+<thead><tr><th>Mature delivery hurdle</th><th>Units</th><th>Hours per unit</th><th>Annual hours</th><th>Direct cost per unit</th></tr></thead>
+<tbody>
+<tr><td>Baseline</td><td>32</td><td>80 analyst + 20 senior</td><td>3,200</td><td>€5.5k</td></tr>
+<tr><td>Recurring account</td><td>24</td><td>10 analyst + 2 senior monthly</td><td>3,456</td><td>€8.1k annually</td></tr>
+<tr><td><strong>Total used</strong></td><td></td><td></td><td><strong>6,656</strong></td><td></td></tr>
+</tbody></table></div>
+<p>Each loaded role must supply 1,450 productive hours from 1,880 paid hours, a 77% saleable-utilisation hurdle. Five roles therefore provide 7,250 hours. Delivery uses 92% of that capacity and leaves 594 hours for rework and peaks. Loaded role costs imply €43 and €59 per productive analyst and senior hour. The €334,000 payroll includes unused capacity, leave and training.</p>
+<p>The €5,500 baseline cost includes €4,600 used role time and €900 specialist and tool allocation. The €8,100 recurring cost includes €6,600 role time and €1,500 specialist and tool allocation. Rounded unit allocations plus paid unused capacity reconcile to the €400,000 direct-cost hurdle.</p>
+<p>Founder compensation of €105,000 is replacement labour for partner sales, quality and management, not surplus. The €255,200 remainder is pre-tax company surplus for reserves, hiring and investment. Recurring revenue is €691,200, or 76% of total revenue. Concentration matters more: two partners sourcing half the clients can renegotiate rates or move the book.</p>
+<p>The following cash bridge is also a design hurdle. It matches eight year-one baselines and four recurring accounts active for six months. The model delays baseline balances by 45 days, rings-fences 21% value-added tax (VAT), and ignores recoverable input VAT. Other cash costs total €46,000. Payroll is founder replacement cost. Capex buys secure workstations, analysis hosts and storage.</p>
+<div class="ledger-wrap"><table class="ledger">
+<thead><tr><th>Month, €000</th><th>Receipts ex VAT</th><th>Closing receivables</th><th>Payroll</th><th>Other cash</th><th>Capex</th><th>VAT paid / reserved</th><th>Capital used</th></tr></thead>
+<tbody>
+<tr><td>1</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>7.1</td><td>6</td><td>12</td><td>0 / 0</td><td>25.1</td></tr>
+<tr><td>2</td><td>3.5</td><td>3.5</td><td>7.1</td><td>4</td><td>0</td><td>0 / 1.5</td><td>33.4</td></tr>
+<tr><td>3</td><td>3.5</td><td>7</td><td>7.1</td><td>3</td><td>0</td><td>0 / 2.9</td><td>40.8</td></tr>
+<tr><td>4</td><td>7</td><td>7</td><td>7.1</td><td>4</td><td>0</td><td>2.9 / 1.5</td><td>44.9</td></tr>
+<tr><td>5</td><td>7</td><td>7</td><td>7.1</td><td>3</td><td>0</td><td>0 / 2.9</td><td>48.0</td></tr>
+<tr><td>6</td><td>7</td><td>7</td><td>7.1</td><td>3</td><td>0</td><td>0 / 4.4</td><td>51.1</td></tr>
+<tr><td>7</td><td>16.6</td><td>7</td><td>7.1</td><td>4</td><td>6</td><td>4.4 / 3.5</td><td><strong>51.6</strong></td></tr>
+<tr><td>8</td><td>16.6</td><td>7</td><td>7.1</td><td>4</td><td>0</td><td>0 / 7.0</td><td>46.1</td></tr>
+<tr><td>9</td><td>16.6</td><td>7</td><td>7.1</td><td>4</td><td>0</td><td>0 / 10.5</td><td>40.6</td></tr>
+<tr><td>10</td><td>13.1</td><td>3.5</td><td>7.1</td><td>4</td><td>0</td><td>10.5 / 2.0</td><td>37.8</td></tr>
+<tr><td>11</td><td>13.1</td><td>0</td><td>7.1</td><td>4</td><td>0</td><td>0 / 4.0</td><td>35.1</td></tr>
+<tr><td>12</td><td>9.6</td><td>0</td><td>7.1</td><td>3</td><td>0</td><td>0 / 6.0</td><td>35.6</td></tr>
+</tbody></table></div>
+<p>The maximum model cash draw is €51,600 in month seven, including €18,000 capex and VAT funding on unpaid invoices. At maturity, €18,700 receivables, €3,900 associated VAT and one month of €55,000 cash costs require €77,600. That leaves €22,400 below the reader's €100,000 ceiling.</p>
+<p>The bridge works only with deposits, advance recurring payment and hiring after booked work. Two-month terms on recurring fees alone create €115,200 of receivables and fail the capital ceiling. The contract must prohibit “pay when paid,” stop work on overdue invoices and keep laboratory spend outside the company.</p>
 <p>Professional liability is also economics. The wholesale price must fund product-security errors-and-omissions cover if available, external legal review of the master subcontract, secure evidence storage, independent peer review for high-severity findings and a cap on aggregate liability. An unlimited indemnity, a conformity warranty, or liability tied to the end customer's machine revenue can make a €7,000 job existential. If a credible partner will buy only on those terms, the model fails rather than “needs better negotiation.”</p>
 </section>
 
