25:<p class="lede">A CE adviser or embedded-development firm already sits beside the machine builder when a product changes. Giving that partner a specialist product-security back office avoids the hardest cold sale and reduces Dutch-language dependence. It also gives away the customer, part of the price and most of the trust. The partner can blame the hidden supplier when a finding is wrong, keep it invisible when the work is good, and hire one engineer after the playbook becomes clear. White-label product security is attractive only if the back office improves the partner's economics while retaining a capability that is hard to internalise.</p>
29:<p>The first buyer is not a manufacturer. It is the managing director or practice lead of a three-to-twenty-five-person Benelux CE/machinery-safety, embedded-software or industrial-automation consultancy. The purchase event is an existing client asking for Cyber Resilience Act (CRA) scope, a software bill of materials (SBOM), secure-development, vulnerability-handling or incident-readiness work. The partner wants to say yes without recruiting a senior product-security specialist before demand is repeatable.</p>
32:<p>The substitute is the partner's senior consultant learning the work, subcontracting a known pentester case by case, reselling a product-security platform, or referring the whole opportunity to a larger specialist. The proposed company must be better than all four while preserving the partner's client relationship. That makes partner economics and trust part of the product, not commercial details to settle later.</p>
42:<p>The distinction between regulation and paid demand is therefore severe. The CRA gives end manufacturers a reason to act. Existing partner programmes show that security is sometimes distributed through advisers. Neither proves that an engineering consultancy will surrender delivery margin, disclose client artefacts and accept dependency on an unknown back office. Only a partner-funded engagement can establish that demand.</p>
52:<p>English delivery can work inside export-oriented engineering teams, while the partner owns Dutch sales, workshops and end-customer documentation. That is a material advantage for the Zaandam-based founder. It is also dependence: the company cannot replace a Dutch-speaking partner quickly after a dispute. The first tests should include one Dutch-led and one cross-border English-led partner to learn whether language or specialist credibility actually drives the channel.</p>
57:<p>This bottom-up model excludes third-party laboratories, certification and legal advice. The partner or end customer contracts them, so they are not pass-through revenue. Every figure below is a design hurdle, not a forecast or observed market price. Baselines collect 50% on booking. Recurring fees are paid monthly in advance.</p>
84:<p>Founder compensation of €105,000 is replacement labour for partner sales, quality and management, not surplus. The €255,200 remainder is pre-tax company surplus for reserves, hiring and investment. Recurring revenue is €691,200, or 76% of total revenue. Concentration matters more: two partners sourcing half the clients can renegotiate rates or move the book.</p>
85:<p>The following cash bridge is also a design hurdle. It matches eight year-one baselines and four recurring accounts active for six months. The model delays baseline balances by 45 days, rings-fences 21% value-added tax (VAT), and ignores recoverable input VAT. Other cash costs total €46,000. Payroll is founder replacement cost. Capex buys secure workstations, analysis hosts and storage.</p>
111:<p><strong>The twentieth end customer</strong>, not twentieth signed partner, is the meaningful scale point. Analysts perform component and evidence work from playbooks; senior engineers review exceptions; the partner runs meetings. The reusable asset is not the customer's SBOM. It is the mapping from common industrial architectures and evidence patterns to review steps, plus a benchmark of hours, questions and failure modes. Customer artefacts remain segregated, exportable and deletable under contract.</p>
129:<p>Channel conflict has four forms. First, the back office approaches the end customer directly. Second, two partners bring the same account. Third, the specialist's platform or another vendor starts selling services to the partner's customer. Fourth, the partner reuses the method with a cheaper subcontractor. The master agreement needs account registration with an expiry, non-solicitation limited in time and scope, rules for inbound end-customer requests, named ownership of derived templates, and a clean exit. These are contract design requirements, not claims that every clause will be enforceable; Dutch counsel must decide the text.</p>
137:<p>The supplier did everything a good channel partner should do: it enabled the channel. That enablement destroyed its information advantage. Its remaining capability—senior exception review—is valuable but episodic, while routine work moves in-house. Customer concentration makes refusal expensive, professional liability remains downstream, and no end customer knows the supplier's name. The company becomes a subcontracting practice with worse pricing power than direct consulting. Good execution loses because the channel captures learning, relationship and margin faster than the back office accumulates a defensible asset.</p>
144:<li><strong>Days 16–30:</strong> have Dutch counsel review a short master subcontract covering manufacturer decisions, confidentiality, account registration, non-solicitation, artefact ownership, incident escalation, liability cap, insurance, payment, audit and exit. Obtain an insurance indication. Spend no more than €7,500 before partner payment, including counsel, insurance advice, travel and secure prototype.</li>
147:<li><strong>Days 76–90:</strong> offer the end customer, through the partner, a six-month recurring unit at €2,400 wholesale per month. The pass rule is two paid rehearsals, one paid live baseline and one partner-backed recurring proposal with at least 35% model gross margin for the partner and 55% for the back office. The kill rule is no live paid baseline after five qualified offers, any required unlimited liability/compliance warranty, or partners consistently withholding the technical access needed for safe delivery.</li>
