+<p>The maximum model cash draw is €51,600 in month seven, including €18,000 capex and VAT funding on unpaid invoices. At maturity, €18,700 receivables, €3,900 associated VAT and one month of €55,000 cash costs require €77,600. That leaves €22,400 below the reader's €100,000 ceiling.</p>
+<p>The bridge works only with deposits, advance recurring payment and hiring after booked work. Two-month terms on recurring fees alone create €115,200 of receivables and fail the capital ceiling. The contract must prohibit “pay when paid,” stop work on overdue invoices and keep laboratory spend outside the company.</p>
 <p>Professional liability is also economics. The wholesale price must fund product-security errors-and-omissions cover if available, external legal review of the master subcontract, secure evidence storage, independent peer review for high-severity findings and a cap on aggregate liability. An unlimited indemnity, a conformity warranty, or liability tied to the end customer's machine revenue can make a €7,000 job existential. If a credible partner will buy only on those terms, the model fails rather than “needs better negotiation.”</p>
 </section>
 
