+1. Rebuilt the mature economics from delivery units: 100 hours per baseline, 12 hours per recurring account-month, four €62k loaded analysts, one €86k loaded senior, 1,450 productive hours per role, 92% planned capacity use, €30k specialist review and €36k tools. Added separate €18k launch capex and a twelve-month receivables/payroll/VAT bridge peaking at €51.6k; the mature stress reserve is €77.6k, below the €100k ceiling. The verdict remains **TEST THROUGH A PARTNER** because the design clears the gate only with deposits, recurring prepayment and staged hiring; sixty-day recurring terms breach the ceiling and remain a kill condition.
+2. Moved `02-cra-roles` directly after the Articles 21–22 proposition and created `02-subcontract-role` as an explicit inference from the statutory definitions, requiring engagement-specific counsel.
+3. Named DEKRA, Bureau Veritas Cybersecurity and TÜV Rheinland from their own service pages; compared delivery scope, named assurance or certification position, and direct customer route against the proposed subcontract model.
+4. Replaced the Cybellum–Hitachi “proves” claim with the limited conclusion that the first-party announcement shows vendors market the combination, without establishing delivery, adoption or channel economics; separately receipted Cybellum's partner programme.
+5. Split all three over-length sentences, clarified that the survey ran in 2024 and was first published in 2025, expanded Cyber Resilience Act, software bill of materials, Product Security Incident Response Team and IEC 62443-4-1 at first use, and removed or expanded other avoidable acronyms. No sentence now exceeds 40 words.
+6. Confirmed the working file already used the current footer, “Seven candidates. One second engine. Evidence before commitment,” and retained it unchanged.
